Title: Diagnosis
Details: The failing test `testConstructorTooLow` attempts to create an `ElitisticListPopulation` instance with an elitism rate of -0.25. According to the test annotation, this should throw an `OutOfRangeException` for the invalid elitism rate below 0. However, examining the constructor of `ElitisticListPopulation(int populationLimit, double elitismRate)`, we see it assigns the elitism rate directly without any validation checks:
```java
public ElitisticListPopulation(final int populationLimit, final double elitismRate) {
    super(populationLimit);
    this.elitismRate = elitismRate;
}
```
In contrast, the setter method `setElitismRate(double elitismRate)` includes a proper range check for the elitism rate between 0 and 1, throwing an `OutOfRangeException` if the value is out of bounds:
```java
public void setElitismRate(final double elitismRate) {
    if (elitismRate < 0 || elitismRate > 1) {
        throw new OutOfRangeException(LocalizedFormats.ELITISM_RATE, elitismRate, 0, 1);
    }
    this.elitismRate = elitismRate;
}
```
The test fails because the constructor does not enforce the same range check as the setter method, allowing invalid invalid elitism rate values like -0.25 to be set without exceptions. The current bug report aligns with this observation, noting the lack of range checks on elitismRate in the constructor. The recent bug reports have no relevant information.

The root cause is the missing validation in the constructor, which leads to the failure of the test expecting an `OutOfRangeException` for an invalid elitism rate passed during construction of `ElitisticListPopulation`.

The fix would be to add the same or equivalent range validation logic in the constructor before assigning the elitism rate field.

